;;; DRAFT -- filter!
;;; Edited 2026-08-03 by Priya.
;;; AUTHORED, not model-seeded. The drafting batch
;;; (scratch/pairs/draft_records.py, see draft_run.log) skipped
;;; this verb because an edited draft already existed, so no raw
;;; model output for it survives. Claiming model provenance here
;;; would be provenance nobody can check.
;;; Structure, examples, row programs, caveats and provenance are
;;; authored, not generated.
;;;
;;; Every :note is the literal placeholder PENDING-ENGINE. NOTHING in this
;;; file was run. The notes are filled by a deterministic script once the
;;; cons-cell engine lands. A printed value here would be a value nobody ran.
;;;
;;; Spec basis: ../spec-pack/filter-bang.slatl -- read ../spec-pack/00-SOURCES.md first,
;;; the normative SRFI-1 text is NOT in this repo.

(verb
  :name "filter!"
  :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "higher-order"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "state-change"
  :signature "(filter! pred lst) -> list"
  :summary "The elements of lst satisfying pred, kept by unlinking the ones that fail rather than by copying the ones that pass. The argument is destroyed."
  :explanation "filter builds a fresh list of the elements that pass. filter! keeps the passing cells where they are and re-points the cdrs to skip the failing ones. The suite's check at list.sld:484-490 is worth reading closely, because it is weaker than the one take! gets: it gathers the argument's cells first and then requires every cell of the result to have been among them. It does not assert that the result is eq? to the argument, and it cannot -- if the first element fails, the head cell is not in the result at all. The bang is a licence to destroy. After the call the cells that made up the argument have been re-used, so the variable you passed in no longer names what it named. Bind the result and read only that. That is a sharper warning here than elsewhere: with take! the argument variable at least still points at the head of the answer, but with filter! it may point at a dropped cell whose cdr now skips ahead into the middle of the result. There is no sense in which the old variable is a prefix or a stub. It is a fragment."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"
     :code "(define xs (list 0 7 8 8 43 -4))\n(display (filter! even? xs))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate"
     :code ";; nothing is allocated: every cell of the result came from xs.\n(define xs (list 0 7 8 8 43 -4))\n(define cells (pair-fold cons (list) xs))\n(define kept (filter! even? xs))\n(display (list 'kept kept 'no-new-cells (every (lambda (c) (memq c cells)) (pair-fold cons (list) kept))))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"
     :code ";; when the first element fails, the argument variable is left naming a\n;; cell that is not in the result at all.\n(define xs (list 7 8 9 10))\n(define kept (filter! even? xs))\n(display (list 'kept kept 'head-was-dropped (not (eq? kept xs))))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
  )
  :caveats ("The argument is destroyed. list.sld:484-490 asserts every cell of the result came from the argument -- nothing is allocated."
             "The result is NOT necessarily eq? to the argument. When the first element fails the predicate, the argument's head cell is dropped and the old variable names a fragment."
             "The order of the kept elements is preserved."
             "pred is called on every element, unlike take-while! which stops early."
             "remove! is filter! with the predicate negated; the two share an implementation and a hazard.")
  :drawbacks ("The leftover binding is neither a prefix nor a stub, which makes the damage harder to describe than for any other verb here."
             "It saves allocation but not traversal, and the pure filter is already a single pass."
             "Because the head may or may not survive, code that both filters destructively and keeps the old name is wrong in a way that depends on the data.")
  :usecases ("Compacting a large freshly-built list in place inside a producer."
             "Removing dead entries from a private worklist between passes."
             "Memory-constrained pipelines where the filtered-out cells must be reclaimed immediately.")
  :related ("filter"
             "remove!"
             "delete!"
             "partition"
             "delete-duplicates!")
  :learn (:concept "Filtering by unlinking, and the fragment left behind"
          :prerequisites ("filter" "set-cdr!" "pair-fold" "eq?")
          :progression "Filter purely first. Then draw the cells on paper and trace which cdr changes when an element in the middle is dropped, and which changes when the first one is.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 linear-update procedures; conformance evidence scratch/r7rs-conformance/larceny/list.sld:484-490; signature derived from the filter record in scheme/reference/33-higher-order.slat. Drafted 2026-08-03; NOT RUN."
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define xs (list 0 7 8 43 -4))\n(display (filter! even? xs))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define a (list 0 7 8 43 -4))\n(define b (list 0 7 8 43 -4))\n(display (equal? (filter even? a) (filter! even? b)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 7 8 9 10))\n(define kept (filter! even? xs))\n(display (list 'head-dropped (not (eq? kept xs))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator wants to compact a large list they built, dropping the entries that fail a test. Candidates: filter (fresh list, source intact), remove with a negated predicate (same, and one negation to get wrong), or filter! (no allocation, source destroyed). The wrinkle that decides it is not speed: with filter! the operator must ALSO stop using the old variable, and unlike take! there is no version of the old variable that is still useful."
     :explanation "eng: the compacted list comes back and the old name is unusable. math: filter! agrees with filter for every predicate and proper list. cs: whether the head survives depends on the first element, so no single statement about the old variable is true for all inputs."
     :meta "The verbs in this family differ in what they leave behind, and filter! leaves behind the least describable thing in the set. That alone is an argument for the pure form.")
    (:eng "If you cannot write down what the old variable means afterwards, do not keep the old variable."
     :math "filter and filter! are the same function of their arguments; only the survivors differ."
     :cs "A result that is sometimes eq? to the argument and sometimes not is the worst case for a caller trying to reason.")
  )
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Removing the entries that fail a test by unlinking them, rather than by copying the ones that pass."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(display (filter! even? (list 1 2 3 4)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a rake with defective wagons cut out" :code "(define rake (list 'good 'defective 'good 'good))\n(display (filter! (lambda (w) (eq? w 'good)) rake))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a playlist with unavailable tracks skipped" :code "(define playlist (list 'ok 'gone 'ok))\n(display (filter! (lambda (t) (eq? t 'ok)) playlist))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, the failing cells are bypassed by re-pointing the cdr before them. Cutting defective wagons out of a rake couples the good wagons on either side directly together; the defective ones are on the ground, not in a second train. A playlist that skips unavailable tracks does the same to an ordering. All three share a removal that closes the gap rather than leaving one."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever removal must preserve the order and adjacency of what remains.")
    (:eng "Closing the gap is the whole job; a removal that leaves a hole is a different operation."
     :math "Filtering preserves order, which is what distinguishes it from selection into a set."
     :cs "This is the linked-list deletion pattern, and the reason it needs the cell BEFORE the one being dropped.")
  )
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define kept (filter! even? (list 1 2 3 4)))\n(display (list 'kept kept 'len (length kept)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (partition-law? p xs)\n  (= (length xs) (+ (length (filter p xs)) (length (remove p xs)))))\n(display (list (partition-law? even? (list 1 2 3 4)) (partition-law? even? (list)) (partition-law? even? (list 2 4))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 0 7 8))\n(define cells (pair-fold cons (list) xs))\n(define kept (filter! even? xs))\n(display (every (lambda (c) (memq c cells)) (pair-fold cons (list) kept)))\n(newline)"
     :claim "filter! returns the elements of lst satisfying pred, in their original order, in a list built entirely from lst's own cells. It is equal? to (filter pred lst). It is eq? to lst exactly when the first element of lst satisfies pred."
     :invariant "forall pred, proper lst. equal?(filter!(pred, lst), filter(pred, lst)) and every cell of filter!(pred, lst) is a cell of lst and |filter(pred,lst)| + |remove(pred,lst)| = |lst|"
     :meta "The pattern is order-preserving selection with no allocation. What would falsify the claim: a fresh cell in the result, a reordering, or a length that does not complement remove. The cell-membership program is the one that distinguishes filter! from filter at all -- every equal? test passes on both.")
    (:eng "The cell-membership check is the only assertion that can tell the two verbs apart. Write it or you have not tested this verb."
     :math "filter and remove partition the list, and the lengths must add up; that is the cheapest sanity law available."
     :cs "pair-fold over both lists is the standard technique for asserting no allocation, and it is why pair-fold belongs in this same set of records.")
  )
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (compact! xs) (filter! (lambda (x) x) xs))\n(display (compact! (list 1 #f 2 #f 3)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (both p xs) (list (filter p xs) (remove p xs)))\n(display (both even? (list 1 2 3 4)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (count-matching p xs) (length (filter p xs)))\n(display (count-matching even? (list 1 2 3 4)))\n(newline)"
     :base "filter! keeps the passing cells and unlinks the failing ones."
     :emergent "eng: compaction, dropping false entries from a sparse list. math: pairing filter with remove gives both halves, and that pairing is what partition does in one pass -- which is the verb to reach for when you want both, because filter! has already destroyed the list the remove would need. cs: counting matches never needs the list at all, and noticing that is often the fix for code that was reaching for a destructive filter to save memory."
     :meta "The family is selection. What emerges is the same constraint seen with take-while!: any composition that needs the source twice rules out the destructive form, and partition exists precisely because that composition is common.")
    (:eng "When you want both sides, ask for both sides. Filtering twice is two passes and, destructively, impossible."
     :math "partition is filter and remove fused; the fusion is what makes it usable where the destructive form is not."
     :cs "Counting or summing over a filter never needs the intermediate list, and folding is the honest way to say so.")
  )
)
